docs/page-templates.md:5:Every wiki page starts with YAML frontmatter. Fields vary by page type but always include `type`, `updated`, `sources`, and `audience`.
docs/page-templates.md:7:## Source page (`wiki/sources/<cite-key>.md`)
docs/page-templates.md:12:raw_handle: FM_3750310
docs/page-templates.md:13:cite_key: he2013-shanghai-cadmium
docs/page-templates.md:20:evidence_tier: A
docs/page-templates.md:22:raw_path: raw/markdown/FM_3750310/FM_3750310.md
docs/page-templates.md:38:Two to four sentences of un-headed opening prose summarizing what the authors measured, what they found, and why it matters. The prose sits directly under the `# H1` title with no `## TL;DR` heading; the wiki reads as authoritative reference, not as a list of casual TL;DRs. Source pages created before 2026-05-08 may still carry the legacy heading; remove it on touch.
docs/page-templates.md:128:Stub pages carry only sections 2 (body table, all rows marked data gap) and 8 (source legend with empty-corpus message). When a stub crosses two-plus contributing A-tier sources, the section-completion subprocess action drafts sections 1, 3, 4, 5, 6, and 7 from the routing audit and contributing source pages.
docs/page-templates.md:219:Coverage cells use `n=<count>` when `n_studies > 0` and `data gap` when `n_studies = 0` with `status: populated`. Numerical cells use `—` for data gaps. Key-sources cells cite the top two or three contributing sources using numbered-alias convention. Per-page tail percentile (formerly `p95 (ppb)` column) stays staff-side per CLAUDE.md Part 19; the `p95_ppb` field remains in machine-readable frontmatter but does not surface on the public page. This 5-column table is a sanctioned exception to the multi-column-tables-discouraged guidance in [[docs/writing-style]].
docs/page-templates.md:245:Row-fit is a compound classification with two axes: **matrix axis** (milk-based vs soy-based vs hydrolyzed; rice-based vs non-rice; root vegetable vs leafy green) and **format axis** (powdered vs ready-to-feed vs concentrated liquid; dry vs as-consumed; jar vs pouch). Each axis is exact, partial, or unknown independently. Source-page TL;DR and frontmatter `matrices` / `products` should record both axes explicitly when known.
docs/page-templates.md:253:Inline and table citations on product-category pages use numbered aliases `[[sources/<cite-key>|1]]`. A `Sources` block at the end maps each number to the full source page.
docs/page-templates.md:320:WikiBiome federation: use `wikibiome_crosswalk` slugs in frontmatter as stable anchors. Prefer mechanistic framing: exposure → microbiome change → functional consequence → disease process. Name taxa at genus or species level where the source supports; do not aggregate up to phylum unless the source did. Keep self-contained enough to lift without the rest of this wiki as context.
docs/page-templates.md:355:- Keep ingredient frontmatter machine-readable and consistent. The app parses these directly.
docs/page-templates.md:362:Every substantive page carries `audience: [regulator, educator, consumer, app]` in frontmatter. Within long pages, section tagging is allowed:
docs/ingest-workflows.md:9:The frontmatter the model fills on each source page is the input to the routing layer described in CLAUDE.md Part 5b. Routing fan-out, ingredient `contamination_profile` updates, and product-page table regeneration all depend on this frontmatter being accurate and at the right level of specificity.
docs/ingest-workflows.md:15:The **filesystem handle** is the `FM_XXXXXXX` identifier. Stable, globally unique, collision-free. Used for `raw/markdown/` folder names, the `raw_handle` frontmatter field, and cross-references that need to be unambiguous across Claude sessions. Claude must never change or re-derive a filesystem handle.
docs/ingest-workflows.md:19:When the triage manifest provides a cite-key, use it. When it does not, derive one from first author, year, and a short topic slug, then record it in source-page frontmatter. If two papers generate the same cite-key, append `-a`, `-b`, etc. Prefer the manifest's pre-populated cite-key over re-deriving on the fly, to prevent inconsistency across sessions.
docs/ingest-workflows.md:21:### Source page creation
docs/ingest-workflows.md:23:Every ingested file produces exactly one source page at `wiki/sources/<cite-key>.md`. Frontmatter includes both identifiers:
docs/ingest-workflows.md:26:raw_handle: FM_3750310
docs/ingest-workflows.md:27:cite_key: he2013-shanghai-cadmium
docs/ingest-workflows.md:38:For **near-duplicates** (papers flagged in the manifest's cluster column), prefer the higher-evidence-tier version. Published versions beat preprints; peer-reviewed versions beat conference abstracts. Create one source page for the preferred version. Note the relationship in frontmatter:
docs/ingest-workflows.md:44:### Pre-populated frontmatter
docs/ingest-workflows.md:49:raw_handle: FM_XXXXXXX
docs/ingest-workflows.md:50:cite_key: <derived>
docs/ingest-workflows.md:65:Source-page frontmatter is the routing layer's input. The model is responsible for declaring `products:` and `ingredients:` against the **existing** wiki taxonomy, not inventing slugs from the paper's text. Before writing any source page, check `wiki/products/` and `wiki/ingredients/` to know what slugs exist. Declared slugs are selected from that vocabulary; new slugs are not invented mid-ingest.
docs/ingest-workflows.md:67:Empty `products: []` on a food or personal-care paper is a defect. A paper that measures heavy metals in any food product, personal-care product, dietary supplement, cosmetic, or any matrix that maps to a consumer-product category must declare at least one slug in `products:`. The one exception: if the natural match is a category Karen has not yet locked (no Step 0 Lock, no scaffolded row pages), the source declares `products: []` AND sets `pending_category_lock: <category-name>` in frontmatter. The routing audit picks the source up after the lock lands. Without the flag, the routing-discipline lint (`tools/audit-routing-discipline.mjs`) flags the source as a Phase 1 defect.
docs/ingest-workflows.md:69:Broad-scope tokens are preferred over invented row-level slugs when the paper does not resolve the row split. If a paper measures "infant formula" generally without specifying powdered vs RTF vs soy vs non-soy, declare `products: [infant-formula]`, not `products: [infant-formula-powder-non-soy]`. The routing audit's `broadProductScopes` vocabulary fans broad scopes out via `route_kind: broad_formula_context`. Overspecifying frontmatter is worse than under-specifying it.
docs/ingest-workflows.md:71:When the model writes the source page, it does not auto-create destination pages. Sources referencing an ingredient slug that doesn't exist surface as a `routing_unresolved.csv` entry; the autonomy loop's `tools/autonomy/create-missing-pages.mjs` creates ingredient stubs at frequency >= 2. Sources referencing a product slug that doesn't exist also surface in `routing_unresolved.csv` but the autonomy loop does **not** create the product stub — product pages are HMTc governance units requiring Karen's Step 0 Lock plus a scaffold-script commit. See CLAUDE.md Part 10 and `docs/order-of-operations.md` for the workflow.
docs/ingest-workflows.md:79:Before ingest into a new HMTc category begins, the session verifies four things: the category's Step 0 Lock document exists, the scaffold script has created the row pages in `wiki/products/`, `wiki/products/index.md` includes a section for the category, and the master rollup page exists. If any of these are missing, the ingest defers all sources touching the category by setting `pending_category_lock: <category-name>` in source frontmatter and leaving `products: []`. The routing layer picks the source up after the lock lands. The model does not invent row-level slugs for not-yet-locked categories; doing so produces governance drift. See `docs/order-of-operations.md` Phase 0.
docs/ingest-workflows.md:83:For this build, ingest means compiling a source into the persistent wiki system. It does not mean "the file is searchable now." The work is split across actors: **model** writes prose (source pages, narrative wiki content where appropriate); **system** scripts handle routing and structured-evidence emission; **generators** rebuild product-page tables and other compiled views; **Karen** decides scope, names new pages, and approves limit-setting work.
docs/ingest-workflows.md:88:2. [model] create or update the canonical source page at `wiki/sources/<cite-key>.md`, filling in frontmatter accurately (especially `products`, `ingredients`, `matrices`, `metals`, `jurisdictions`).
docs/ingest-workflows.md:91:5. [model, on Karen's approval] create a stub page if the destination doesn't exist (a `routing_unresolved` ingredient or product). The model proposes; Karen approves; the model creates the stub with required frontmatter.
docs/ingest-workflows.md:95:If automation cannot place evidence cleanly, do not silently drop the source. The routing audit emits `routing_unresolved.csv` and `routing_malformed.csv` when source frontmatter does not connect or is malformed; the structured-evidence reingest queue flags value-level extraction work. Missing placements stay visible and recoverable.
docs/ingest-workflows.md:99:When Karen drops a file into `raw/` and says "ingest this", the model's deliverable is the source page plus a list of any new pages the routing layer flags as unresolved. Routing fan-out, ingredient `contamination_profile` updates, and product-page table regeneration are downstream system passes, not part of the ingest itself.
docs/ingest-workflows.md:103:3. [model] Create the source page using the template in [[docs/page-templates]]. Frontmatter accuracy is load-bearing because it feeds the routing layer; fill `products`, `ingredients`, `matrices`, `metals`, `jurisdictions` at the level of specificity the source actually states.
docs/ingest-workflows.md:104:4. [system] Rebuild the routing audit by running `npm run evidence:source-routes` (or letting the watcher pick up the new source page). The audit names the affected product, ingredient, metal, and regulation pages.
docs/ingest-workflows.md:120:4. Commit after each batch of 200, not per-paper. A single commit per batch: `ingest batch 03 (papers 401-600): 47 source pages added`.
docs/ingest-workflows.md:123:   - New source pages created (list of cite-keys)
docs/ingest-workflows.md:130:**The model's only output during bulk ingest is source pages.** Bulk ingest sessions do not edit product-category page tables, do not edit `data/evidence/product_source_routing_audit.csv`, do not edit `data/evidence/values.jsonl`, and do not advance `contamination_profile` blocks on ingredient pages. Those layers are downstream artifacts. If a bulk session finds itself wanting to update a product page or routing audit by hand, that is a signal to stop and surface the misunderstanding to Karen.
docs/ingest-workflows.md:132:Frontmatter accuracy on source pages is load-bearing because it feeds the routing layer. A source that does not split soy vs non-soy formula gets `matrices: [infant-formula]`, not `matrices: [infant-formula-powder-non-soy]`. Overspecifying to "pre-decide" routing is worse than leaving the routing layer to handle fan-out.
docs/ingest-workflows.md:139:4. File the answer as a page by default under `wiki/queries/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<slug>.md` with frontmatter linking to drawn-from pages. The exception: throwaway lookups that are obviously one-shot (e.g., "what's the cite key for the Abt 2018 paper?") — don't file those.
docs/ingest-workflows.md:166:- If a figure contains data we need structured (a concentration distribution), transcribe it into a markdown table on the source page.
docs/conventions.md:23:**Frontmatter wikilinks:** Page-reference arrays in frontmatter use the quoted-wikilink form `["[[folder/slug]]", "[[folder/slug]]"]`. Quoting is required because YAML reserves `[` as a flow-sequence delimiter; without quotes, `[[` is parsed as a nested empty list. Wrapping page-reference arrays as wikilinks is what makes Obsidian's graph view surface relationships sourced from frontmatter.
docs/conventions.md:33:All other frontmatter array fields stay bare; they are tag/classifier vocabularies (`metals`, `jurisdictions`, `audience`, `species`, `drivers`, `aliases`, `mechanism`, `disease_links`, `matrices`, `applicable_regulations`, `typical_ingredients`, `near_duplicates`), not page references.
docs/conventions.md:35:Unresolved-target wikilinks (frontmatter or body wikilinks pointing to pages that do not yet exist) are intentional backlog markers, not lint errors. They render as dotted nodes in Obsidian's graph and indicate where the wiki is expected to grow next.
docs/conventions.md:49:Every source gets an `evidence_tier`.
docs/conventions.md:84:## [2026-04-24] batch | batch-03 (papers 401-600) — 200 source pages ingested
docs/conventions.md:85:Source pages: 200 created. Routing rows added by the audit refresh: 162. New-page proposals: 4. Synthesis triggers fired downstream: 11 cells crossed the second-source threshold and are queued for the synthesis pass per CLAUDE.md Part 9. Batch report: [[batch-reports/batch-03]].
docs/conventions.md:89:The bulk-ingest session's deliverable is the source pages and the new-page proposals. The routing audit refresh, the synthesis pass, and the product-page generator runs are downstream system passes that execute after the batch commits.
CLAUDE.md:38:The Paleo Foundation operates from Cyprus. EU database-rights law provides stronger protection for curated databases than US equivalents. This is a structural advantage to design around, not incidental. Terms of service, access controls, and commercial licensing will leverage this. Claude sessions should preserve per-claim provenance (which source page supports which wiki claim) so that if any source is later withdrawn or contested, the wiki can be re-derived from its source pages without silent data contamination.
CLAUDE.md:90:This build is a compiled wiki, not a query-time RAG surface. Queries may read raw files when needed, but the default goal of ingest is to compile durable knowledge once into source pages, evidence registers, routed wiki pages, and generated public outputs so the same work does not need to be rediscovered from scratch on every question.
CLAUDE.md:98:**Version control.** The wiki is in git. Commit after every ingest, query, lint, or schema change. Commit messages follow the `log.md` format (see [[docs/conventions]]). Git history is the versioning mechanism; the `updated:` frontmatter field is a convenience, not the source of truth. When a wiki page is cited in future HMT&C documentation, litigation response, or regulatory submission, it must be possible to reconstruct exactly what the page said on a specific date. Do not skip commits because "nothing much changed." For bulk ingest runs, commit after each batch of 200 papers rather than per-paper; batch commits with descriptive batch reports preserve reconstructibility without overwhelming the log.
CLAUDE.md:104:See [[docs/ingest-workflows]] for source-file handling: filesystem-handle vs cite-key, source-page creation, deduplication, pre-populated frontmatter, and existing-pages-aware ingest discipline (including the Phase 1 rule that empty `products: []` on a food/personal-care paper is a defect).
CLAUDE.md:114:The routing layer is the answer to a recurring failure mode: papers get ingested with frontmatter that correctly names the products and ingredients they touch, but no downstream process reads that frontmatter and fans the source out to the sibling pages. Without the routing layer, every later session has to re-derive routing from scratch, which is slow and lossy.
CLAUDE.md:118:`data/evidence/product_source_routing_audit.csv` is the canonical routing artifact. One row per (source, target-page, route_kind) triple. The `route_kind` vocabulary is fixed: `direct_evidence`, `broad_formula_context`, `broad_powder_context`, `broad_product_context`, `exposure_only`, `regulatory_context`. Two sibling logs catch failures: `data/evidence/routing_unresolved.csv` (source frontmatter does not connect to any known target page) and `data/evidence/routing_malformed.csv` (source frontmatter is missing required fields).
CLAUDE.md:124:1. File watcher: a `chokidar` watcher on `wiki/sources/` triggers a rebuild within seconds of any source page being created or modified. Runs as part of `npm run serve`.
CLAUDE.md:130:The model writes the source page. It fills in frontmatter accurately, especially the `products`, `ingredients`, `matrices`, `metals`, and `jurisdictions` arrays, because those arrays are the routing layer's input. If a source touches infant formula generally without specifying soy vs non-soy, the frontmatter should say `matrices: [infant-formula]`, not `matrices: [infant-formula-powder-non-soy]`. The routing layer fans broad scopes out to sibling pages; the model does not pre-decide that fan-out by overspecifying frontmatter.
CLAUDE.md:136:The routing layer's output feeds the synthesis workflow in Part 9. When the audit reports that an (ingredient, metal) cell has accumulated a new contributing source, the synthesis pass reads the contributing sources together and updates the ingredient page's `contamination_profile`. The model writes source pages during ingest and runs synthesis as a separate workflow; the routing audit is what connects the two.
CLAUDE.md:150:See [[docs/page-templates]] (Audience layering section). Every substantive page carries `audience: [regulator, educator, consumer, app]` in frontmatter; section-level `<!-- audience: ... -->` tagging allows one wiki to render three ways. Consumer translation must specify dose, population, and consumption frequency; never use undefined qualifiers ("small," "trace," "safe," "dangerous") without a quantitative anchor.
CLAUDE.md:158:Synthesis is the workflow that turns accumulated source-page evidence into the `contamination_profile` values on ingredient pages, the narrative summaries on metal pages, and the cross-source claims in regulation pages. **It is a workflow, not an ingest step.** Source pages get written during ingest; synthesis happens later, as a dedicated pass triggered either by the routing audit signaling that an ingredient/metal cell has new evidence or by Karen requesting it.
CLAUDE.md:176:- **Initial population** (first two sources): read both source pages in full; compute `typical_ppb`, `p95_ppb`, `n_studies: 2`, `confidence: low`; update the cell to `status: populated`; log the event to `data/evidence/review_events.jsonl`.
CLAUDE.md:204:Published third-party testing (Consumer Reports, HBBF, etc.) can be cited as sources, and their methodology can be discussed, but do not reproduce their brand-level tables into the wiki. Link to the source page instead.
CLAUDE.md:218:See [[docs/conventions]] for units (always ppb µg/kg unless source uses different units; wet vs dry weight always specified), metal names (Pb, Cd, iAs, tAs, MeHg, tHg, Ni, Al, Cr, Cr-VI, Sn, Sb, U with iAs/tAs and tHg/MeHg as non-negotiable distinctions), HMT&C analyte vocabulary (the official 10-analyte certification list: Pb, tAs, Cd, MeHg, tHg, iAs, Ni, Al, Cr-VI, Sn), dates (ISO-8601), cite-keys, wikilink frontmatter rules, numbers/regulatory-values/uncertainty discipline, and consumer language.
CLAUDE.md:234:Lint runs across the wiki and checks: contradictions, stale claims, orphans, missing/malformed frontmatter, regulatory-value drift, silent threshold deviation (the Part 2 wiki/HMT&C drift check), evidence-tier mismatch, provenance gaps, the full routing-audit family (unresolved, malformed, stale, orphaned reference, missing-products, zero-routed-source, Step 0 Lock pending), the ingredient coverage/body-table/mandatory-section audits, the product-page mandatory-section audit, and the language-judgment checks (em dashes, inline bold, prose-vs-bullets, undefined consumer qualifiers) that run as the delegated sweep in [[docs/tasks/prose-style-sweep]]. Results are written to `wiki/lint/<YYYY-MM-DD>-lint.md`; log entries use `op: lint`. Routing lint failures block builds, not just produce reports, because routing drift propagates silently.
CLAUDE.md:299:Schema mirrors this wiki's conventions (frontmatter, evidence tiers, cite keys).

